WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
This is The Spread, your energy
markets briefing for Wednesday,

2
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.600
the twenty-first of October.

3
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.320
This episode is written with AI,
and read by an AI clone of my voice.

4
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.893
Iranian strikes in the Gulf are
doing heavier damage to ships,

5
00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:17.573
and supertanker rates have now
passed a million dollars a day.

6
00:00:17.627 --> 00:00:21.120
Producers are cutting crude
prices to hold on to buyers.

7
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.440
In Europe, a sharp fall in Norwegian
gas output and a near-stall in storage

8
00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.160
injections are tightening an
already thin winter balance.

9
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.040
On the markets. Brent was cited at
one hundred and eight dollars a barrel

10
00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.000
on Tuesday, with West Texas Intermediate
about five dollars under it,

11
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.120
according to OilPrice dot com.

12
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.920
Asian liquefied natural gas,
priced on the Japan Korea Marker,

13
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.360
rose above thirty dollars per
million British thermal units.

14
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.480
That's its highest since
December twenty twenty-two.

15
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.600
EU gas storage stood at seventy-four
point six percent full on the nineteenth

16
00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.800
of October, according to
Gas Infrastructure Europe.

17
00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.720
Starting in the Gulf. Iranian attacks
are hitting more ships and doing

18
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.240
more severe damage than
earlier in the conflict,

19
00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:07.560
according to gCaptain. Engine
rooms are increasingly the target.

20
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.960
A ship with a wrecked engine room
stays out of service far longer than

21
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.480
one with hull or deck damage, which
takes tonnage out of a fleet that

22
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.040
was already short.

23
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.800
Freight shows the strain.

24
00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.680
OilPrice dot com reports that spot
rates for very large crude carriers

25
00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.120
now exceed a million dollars a day.

26
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.600
The same report says producers are
trimming crude prices so buyers can

27
00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.720
absorb the extra shipping cost.

28
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.840
So the hit is moving off refiners'
delivered costs and onto what producers

29
00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.760
actually realise per barrel.

30
00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.840
Iran's Revolutionary Guard Corps also
claimed on Tuesday that a supertanker

31
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.160
caught fire after striking
a mine south of the strait.

32
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.800
It's the latest in a run of such
claims, and no independent evidence

33
00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.040
has surfaced. Neither report gives
a fresh tally or names the ships hit

34
00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:52.320
since last week.

35
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.840
Staying with Iran, but on the diplomacy.

36
00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.120
Oil slipped back from five-month highs
on Tuesday as uncertain negotiations

37
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:02.080
kept trading volatile,
according to Rigzone.

38
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.240
Unnamed senior officials told gCaptain
that Tehran is deliberately dragging

39
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.160
out the talks to weaken President
Trump before November's midterms.

40
00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.720
That rests on anonymous sourcing,
and Tehran hasn't said anything of

41
00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.920
the kind publicly. If it's right,
relief on the Gulf risk premium or

42
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.400
on Iranian barrels is further off.

43
00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.800
The gas side of the same story is in Asia.

44
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:27.280
That multi-year high in LNG comes on
the back of repeated tanker attacks

45
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.640
around the Middle East and
Europe's worsening winter outlook,

46
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.480
OilPrice dot com reports.

47
00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.720
China and Japan are buying eight
to ten percent less LNG than a year

48
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.600
ago. Even so, that restraint
hasn't been enough to cap prices.

49
00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.400
Turning to European gas. Norwegian gas
output fell twelve point six percent

50
00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.800
in September from August, according
to the Norwegian Offshore Directorate.

51
00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.640
Total petroleum production averaged
about three point seven seven million

52
00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.800
barrels of oil equivalent a day.

53
00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.360
That's down ten point
four percent on the month,

54
00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.720
and four percent below the
directorate's own forecast.

55
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.440
The reports give no cause for the drop.

56
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.080
Norway is Europe's
largest pipeline supplier,

57
00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.040
so any shortfall has
to be covered with LNG,

58
00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.720
and that means bidding against
Asia at those elevated prices.

59
00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:16.960
Storage reflects the squeeze.

60
00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.720
Injections nearly stalled, with
storage up just a hundredth of a point

61
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.960
on the nineteenth. A day earlier
it had risen eleven hundredths.

62
00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.520
Two related moves. Equinor has
scaled back electrification at Troll,

63
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.880
Norway's biggest gas field, saying
the original plan would have cost

64
00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.053
production at the wrong
time, Rigzone reports.

65
00:03:36.107 --> 00:03:40.160
And in Egypt, Eni has begun
start-up at Meleiha Phase Two,

66
00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:43.147
designed for up to one hundred
million cubic feet a day,

67
00:03:43.253 --> 00:03:47.760
according to World Oil. More domestic
gas could trim Egypt's LNG imports

68
00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.440
and possibly leave more
cargoes for Europe.

69
00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.680
On to shipping policy, where the
shadow-fleet plan now has a mechanism.

70
00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.160
Energy Secretary Chris Wright said
Washington is authorising the sale

71
00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.760
of sanctioned tankers
to approved investors,

72
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.013
according to Splash twenty-four seven.

73
00:04:03.067 --> 00:04:06.640
The buyers would refurbish them and
return them to legitimate crude trades

74
00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.280
to ease freight costs. For
now that's Wright's account.

75
00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.480
No Treasury terms or vessel
numbers have been published.

76
00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.960
Bloomberg's analysis suggests the
plan may draw in only a sliver of the

77
00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:19.880
dark fleet. It points
to existing contracts,

78
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.240
complex compliance checks
and high asset prices.

79
00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.880
Read that way, any relief for tanker
owners' customers would be modest

80
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.040
and slow.

81
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.080
Over to Russia. Shipments from the
Baltic port of Primorsk stopped for

82
00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.840
several days, and weekly seaborne
crude exports fell by three hundred

83
00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:39.173
and ninety thousand barrels
a day, Bloomberg reports.

84
00:04:39.227 --> 00:04:42.453
That left them at three point
one three million barrels a day,

85
00:04:42.507 --> 00:04:44.200
the lowest since March.

86
00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.600
The four-week average to the eighteenth
of October was three point five

87
00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.160
nine million barrels a day.

88
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.840
That's down a hundred and ten thousand.

89
00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:55.440
Thirty-one tankers loaded nearly
twenty-two million barrels,

90
00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:58.800
against thirty-four tankers and
about twenty-four and a half million

91
00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:00.160
the week before.

92
00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:03.040
There's no explanation
yet for the Primorsk halt.

93
00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.520
Fewer Baltic barrels mean less Urals
crude for buyers in India and Turkey,

94
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.000
and less export revenue for Moscow.

95
00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:14.080
And in the US. The American Petroleum
Institute estimated a crude draw

96
00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:18.320
of four point nine million barrels in
the week to the sixteenth of October.

97
00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:22.400
By its count, commercial crude is down
more than fifty-six million barrels

98
00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:26.480
over twenty-seven weeks. Draws from
the Strategic Petroleum Reserve have

99
00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:29.840
kept total US crude near
where it started the year.

100
00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:34.160
Refiners are cashing in. Citgo raised
its operating profit outlook for

101
00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.680
this year by two billion
dollars on rising fuel margins,

102
00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:40.080
according to a document seen by Bloomberg.

103
00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.680
That's direct evidence Gulf Coast
refiners are capturing the tightness

104
00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:44.960
in products.

105
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.720
A few more things. The Bureau of
Safety and Environmental Enforcement

106
00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:52.240
published its weekly deepwater
activity report on Tuesday.

107
00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.453
Shut-in figures for
Hurricane Isaias, though,

108
00:05:54.507 --> 00:05:57.653
are still missing, five
days after they were due.

109
00:05:57.707 --> 00:06:01.000
What to watch next. The
EIA weekly lands today,

110
00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:03.867
a check on that industry
estimate of a crude draw.

111
00:06:03.973 --> 00:06:07.120
Daily European storage readings
through to the twenty-seventh will show

112
00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:09.680
whether the stall in injections persists.

113
00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:13.413
And the overdue Isaias shut-in
numbers, whenever they appear.

114
00:06:13.467 --> 00:06:17.040
That's the brief. Sources for everything
you heard are in the show notes.
